    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Explain why the indefinite integral $\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx$ cannot be found using the standard form $$\\int x^n\\,dx=\\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Here $n=-1$, so $n+1=0$. The formula would involve division by zero and requires $n\\ne-1$.",
      "worked": "Since $\\frac{1}{x}=x^{-1}$, the exponent is $n=-1$. Substituting into the proposed primitive gives $$\\frac{x^{-1+1}}{-1+1}+C=\\frac{x^0}{0}+C,$$ which is undefined because it involves division by zero. The power rule therefore requires $n\\ne-1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
